+# Urban heat: four-week personal learning guide
+
+This guide distinguishes evidence in the six supplied cards from planning
+choices. The first section reports only card-supported claims; the four-week
+sequence is explicitly a recommendation for how I can work through the inbox.
+
+## Sourced facts
+
+- The pilot placed air-temperature loggers two metres above ground and recorded readings at 15-minute intervals. [S1]
+- Its observations cover 18 summer days in one neighborhood west of the river, with no measurements elsewhere in the city or in another season. [S1]
+- Forty-three adults supplied self-reported sleep diaries during a heat event. [S2]
+- Three of the 12 bus stops audited at midday on one July day had shade over the marked waiting area. [S3]
+- Designated library branches may be activated as cooling centers after the city issues a heat alert, and the emergency manager holds activation authority. [S4]
+- The cool-roof pilot covered 24 occupied apartment buildings and monitored them for six weeks. [S5]
+- Average afternoon indoor temperature in the treated buildings was 1.1 degrees Celsius lower than in matched comparison buildings during that window. [S5]
+- Eight convenience-sample interviews raised multilingual heat notices and predictable maintenance access as renter priorities. [S6]
+
+## Recommendations: four-week guide
+
+The items below are sequencing and study recommendations, not additional
+findings. Each activity is intended to leave behind a small reusable note.
+
+### Week 1 — Measurement
+
+- **Primary reading:** S1 — Block-scale temperature logger pilot
+- **Activity:** Build a measurement-limits card that records the observed place, season, duration, logger height, and sampling interval before listing any possible follow-up question.
+
+### Week 2 — Governance
+
+- **Primary reading:** S4 — Cooling-center activation memo
+- **Activity:** Draw a one-page decision map linking the heat alert, activation authority, and library branches, then add clearly marked blanks for funding and disability-access information.
+
+### Week 3 — Equity
+
+- **Primary reading:** S3 — Transit shade access audit
+- **Activity:** Make a shade-access worksheet that separates the audited stops and time window from the unaudited times and seasons, leaving space for questions about who waits there.
+
+### Week 4 — Interventions
+
+- **Primary reading:** S5 — Cool-roof housing pilot
+- **Activity:** Create an intervention scorecard with the pilot size, comparison, monitoring window, reported outcome, and empty fields for cost and longer-term follow-up.
+
+## Open questions and uncertainty
+
+- How temperatures vary citywide is not established: the source cards do not establish conditions beyond the single neighborhood and summer window in the logger pilot. [S1]
+- Whether bedroom heat caused reported awakenings is not established: the source cards do not establish causality from the observational, self-reported diary study. [S2]
+- Whether activated libraries have sufficient funding and disability access is not established: the source cards do not establish either implementation detail. [S4]
+
